      <h2>Why Do Insurance Companies Give Such Huge Discounts for Bundling?</h2>
      <p>
        Insurers don't hand out 15% to 25% discounts out of generosity. Two business numbers make bundles hugely profitable for them:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Customers Stick Around Twice as Long:</strong> A standalone auto customer shops around and leaves after an average of 3.2 years. Bundle home and auto together, and average tenure jumps to <strong>8.4 years</strong>. Moving two policies at once is a hassle, and carriers bank on that friction.</li>
        <li><strong>Homeowners File Fewer Auto Claims:</strong> On average, homeowners tend to be more financially settled and file fewer costly vehicle claims than the broader driving pool, making them attractive auto customers.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>When Bundling Actually Costs You MORE Money</h2>
      <p>
        Before you assume one carrier is automatically cheapest, watch out for three common situations where splitting wins:
      </p>

      <div class="card" style="margin: 1.5rem 0;">
        <h4 style="color: var(--color-primary); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">The Three Unbundle Traps:</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>You Live in a Catastrophe Weather Zone:</strong> Along coastal Florida, coastal Texas, or wildfire-prone parts of California, a big national carrier might quote $5,500 for home coverage while a regional property specialist quotes $3,600. Saving 15% on an $1,800 auto policy ($270) doesn't come close to making up for overpaying $1,900 on the house.</li>
          <li><strong>You Have a High-Risk Driver in the Household:</strong> If someone on your policy has a recent DUI or multiple at-fault crashes, preferred bundle carriers will hit the auto side with steep surcharges. Placing that driver on a separate non-standard auto policy while keeping your home with a strong mutual insurer often costs less overall.</li>
          <li><strong>The "Affiliate Underwriter" Catch:</strong> Some big auto brands market a home bundle, yet the home policy is actually underwritten by an outside partner like Homesite or American Modern. You end up dealing with two separate claims departments, and single-deductible storm perks usually don't apply.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>The Best Way to Shop for a Multi-Policy Package</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Price your car and house separately first—use our <a href="/guides/auto-insurance/" style="color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600; text-decoration: underline;">national car insurance rate guide</a> and <a href="/guides/home-insurance/" style="color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600; text-decoration: underline;">homeowners insurance replacement cost guide</a> to keep limits consistent.</li>
        <li>Ask each carrier for a bundled quote with the exact same limits and deductibles.</li>
        <li>Compare the true annual household total: (Best Standalone Auto + Best Standalone Home) vs. (Bundled Auto + Bundled Home).</li>
        <li>Check the fine print so a "cheaper" bundle isn't quietly bumping your home deductible up or cutting your personal liability limit down.</li>
      </ol>

