      <h2>Worked Math: When Splitting Beats Bundling by $310/Year</h2>
      <p>
        Shop the <strong>combined annual dollar total</strong>, not the headline percentage off. Take a suburban household with two clean-record drivers and a $420,000 home in a hail-prone ZIP code:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Bundled Quote (Carrier A, 18% Multi-Policy Discount):</strong> Auto policy = $1,920/yr + Homeowners policy = $2,640/yr &rarr; <strong>Combined Total: $4,560/year</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Unbundled Split (Carrier B for Auto + Regional Mutual C for Home):</strong> Standalone Auto = $1,480/yr + Standalone Homeowners = $2,770/yr &rarr; <strong>Combined Total: $4,250/year</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Carrier A waved an 18% discount, yet its base auto rate in that ZIP code ran $440 higher than Carrier B's. Splitting the two policies saves <strong>$310 a year</strong> with the exact same liability limits and deductibles.
      </p>

